{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-363851",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{5\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{5}{5\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{20}{25}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{3}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{5\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{5\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{5}{5\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
